meta {
  name: View the members
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

get {
  url: {{groupService}}/members?groupKey={{groupKeyQuery}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  groupKey: {{groupKeyQuery}}
}

script:pre-request {
  bru.setVar("groupKeyQuery", encodeURIComponent(bru.getEnvVar("groupKey") || ""));
}

tests {
  test("names the live members and what each is sharing", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.members).to.be.an("array");
    expect(res.body.members.map((m) => m.displayName)).to.include(bru.getEnvVar("displayName"));
  });
}

docs {
  # View the members

  Who is in the group, without stating anything.

  Every row is a live member: the id its own secret derives, the name it claimed, and whether it is publishing.
  `publishing` is read off the relay's connection list on every call, so a publish that dropped stops showing without a second request.

  What it never carries is who is watching what. That is the same line the stream index draws, which answers what a member may open and never who else is reading.

  The group key rides in the query here rather than in a body, the way the stream index takes it, a GET having no body a cache or a proxy will honour.
  It is percent-encoded first: a group key is standard base64, and a `+` written into a query is a space by the time a server reads it.
}
